{
  "image_name": "e40c30a3ace89d6a4ffe3620f4999e8a.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts topological and non-topological magnetic textures and links them to different spin patterns and interaction regimes. Across the top, small arrow diagrams show three topological cases—Néel, Bloch, and mixed—versus non-topological swirling states on the right. These cartoons indicate how the in-plane magnetization rotates around a core.\n\nThe larger middle images place these textures in a magnetic background. A compact skyrmion is shown for the Néel case, while larger circular “bubble” domains are shown for Bloch and mixed cases, and a similar-looking but non-topological achiral bubble appears in the non-topological column. Colored arrows around the domain wall emphasize the rotational pattern, while the surrounding gray arrows show the uniform background magnetization. The labels underneath summarize the handedness: skyrmions are homochiral, Bloch-like bubbles can be homo- or heterochiral, and the non-topological bubbles are achiral.\n\nAt the bottom, schematic profiles of the polar angle θ(r) distinguish skyrmions from bubbles. The skyrmion has a narrow peak-like profile reaching π, whereas the bubble shows a broad plateau near π with sharp edges. A final axis suggests a crossover in stabilization mechanisms, from DMI-dominated skyrmions to dipolar-interaction-dominated bubbles.",
